Aston University is undertaking a Preliminary Market Engagement (PME) exercise to inform a future procurement for the provision of Independent Accountant's Reports (IARs) and Grant Assurance Services.
The University wishes to engage with suppliers active in the grant assurance, audit and accountancy sector to better understand market capability, service delivery models, pricing approaches, innovation opportunities, and routes to market.
The anticipated scope may include, but is not limited to:
Independent Accountant's Reports (IARs) for Innovate UK funded projects;
Grant assurance and certification services for UKRI funded projects;
Assurance engagements relating to Horizon Europe and successor programmes;
Agreed-Upon Procedures (AUP) engagements;
Grant assurance requirements arising from charitable, governmental and other research funders;
Related advisory services associated with grant assurance and certification requirements.
The purpose of this engagement is solely to gather market intelligence and obtain supplier feedback to help shape the University's procurement strategy and future tender documentation.
Participation in this PME exercise will not confer any advantage or disadvantage in any subsequent procurement process.

Modified Tests Framework 2027-2031 Pre-Market Engagement
The Standards and Testing Agency (STA), an Executive Agency of the Department for Education (DfE) is looking to re-establish a Modified Tests Framework for the development and delivery of modified versions of National Curriculum Tests for children with visual impairments, hearing impairments and special education needs and disabilities. (SEND) The new framework will be for four years from 1 February 2027 to 31 January 2031.
Successful suppliers will be required to provide modified versions of standard tests in various formats including modified large print and Unified English Braille. Modified versions are required for all national curriculum tests including the following:
Phonics Screening Check (braille version only);
Key stage 1 tests in English Grammar Punctuation and Spelling, English Reading, Mathematics and;
Key stage 2 tests in English Grammar Punctuation and Spelling, English Reading, Mathematics and;
Key stage 3 English Reading.
However, this is subject to change depending on government policy.  The tests required for modification will be specified in each call-off.
Suppliers will also be expected to provide:
assessment reports evaluating suitability of test items including recommendations for modifications for future test development.
amendments to the standard mark scheme guidance to enable consistent marking of the modified versions of the Live tests;
braille proofing and quality assurance of Grade 1 and 2 UEB versions of the national curriculum tests;
a specialist helpline service:
to answer queries from schools and other users of modified tests;
to facilitate on-demand orders of past test Braille materials and the replacements of reception baselines assessment resources
to validate Modified test orders for the incumbent Test Operations Supplier
to facilitate late Modified test orders for the incumbent Test Operations Supplier

Westminster City Council (the Council) is seeking to appoint a single, independent supplier to deliver the Highways Audit & Asset Management (A&AM) Contract.
The supplier will provide audit and compliance services, asset management services and other professional services supporting the Council's Highways & Public Realm Contract. The A&AM supplier will provide objective scrutiny, assurance and challenge and must remain operationally and professionally independent from the Highways & Public Realm contractor.
•	audit of performance information, contractual compliance and delivery against the Highways & Public Realm Contract;
•	commercial and cost assurance, including review of estimates, applications, final accounts, compensation events, Price List items, indexation and volume rebate calculations where specified;
•	technical assurance, inspections, design reviews, defects and compliance audits;
•	asset-management strategy, inventory and data assurance, planned preventative maintenance programme development, BCI calculations and statutory/industry data reporting;
•	responsible procurement, social value and carbon-reduction requirements, including assurance of relevant Highways & Public Realm contractor tender commitments where required by the Scope;
•	use of the Council's IT Solution, including CAMS, HMMS, BSMMS and CMS, to support evidence-based audit and asset-management activity; and
•	additional specialist and professional services instructed through Task Orders within the scope and maximum value of the Contract.
Contractual reporting and improvement requirements include an Audit Plan (the first due no later than one month after the Starting Date and, for subsequent years, a draft due by 1 March), monthly Performance Reports under Schedule 5, and an annual Innovation Report identifying at least five improvement opportunities and quantifying financial, carbon and customer benefit. Further service-specific reports, logs, calculations, programmes and deliverables are set out in the Scope and Performance Management schedule.
The intended form of contract is the NEC4 Term Service Contract. The initial term is three years from 1 April 2027 to 31 March 2030, with up to two optional one-year extensions at the Council's discretion and subject to the Contract.
This procurement follows preliminary market engagement undertaken under UK2 notice 2025/S 000-073888. Participation in that engagement does not confer any advantage in this procurement.

Southern Water is developing a programme to transform and rationalise its data centre estate. This Request for Information (RFI) is a preliminary market engagement exercise under the Procurement Act 2023, intended to inform and shape the emerging vision ahead of any procurement. Through it, Southern Water is seeking to:
•	Understand the capability, capacity and appetite of the market to deliver a programme of this nature;
•	Test the feasibility and realism of the emerging vision, and in particular the delivery timescale;
•	Gather delivery approaches, technical options, innovations, case studies and indicative commercial models;
•	Understand how the market would best package (Lot) the requirement; and
•	Inform Southern Water's business case, sourcing strategy and eventual procurement approach.

Connexus is seeking to appoint a panel of suitably qualified RICS Registered Valuers to deliver a comprehensive range of valuation services across its operating areas of Shropshire and Herefordshire.
The appointed valuers will support Connexus in meeting its operational, regulatory, and strategic requirements by providing accurate, timely, and compliant valuation advice across a diverse property portfolio.
The scope of services will include, but is not limited to:
•Domestic valuations, including Right to Buy (RTB) and Right to Acquire (RTA);
•Commercial valuations, typically relating to small retail units;
•Land and site valuations for development purposes;
•Land valuations associated with small disposals;
•Shared Ownership resale valuations;
•Market rent valuations;
•Market sales valuations;
•Section 106 (1999 Act) valuations;
•Valuations for lease extensions.
In addition, the panel will be required to provide:
•Buyback valuations and support with land/property purchase negotiations;
•Professional advice and Negotiation services to ensure value for money and robust decision-making.
Valuations may be undertaken through a combination of:
•Physical site inspections, and
•Desktop valuation exercises, where appropriate and in line with RICS standards.
The contract will be split into two Lots, Lot 1 covering Shropshire and Lot 2 covering Herefordshire.
In respect of each lot, the top three bidders who receive the highest overall score will be invited to enter into a contract with Connexus.
All lots will be assessed separately. Tenderers can bid for one or more lots and can be awarded a place on one or more Lots. 
Connexus Homes Limited reserves the right to decide to award places on each lot to different provider(s).
Whilst Connexus anticipates that the overall requirement may be split broadly 50/50 between Shropshire (Lot 1) and Herefordshire (Lot 2), this is provided for indicative purposes only. Connexus does not guarantee any proportion of work, value, or volume under either Lot, and instructions will be issued based on operational requirements, property location, supplier expertise, capacity, and availability.

Establishment of a Dynamic Market pursuant to section 34 of the Procurement Act 2023 for the physical supply of electricity and gas to all Authority sites along with associated invoicing and query management services. A Dynamic Market is a procurement mechanism that:
•remains open to new suppliers throughout its duration;
•allows suppliers to apply for admission at any time;
•enables the Authority to conduct competitive selection processes among admitted suppliers

The Home Office anticipates conducting a procurement for an end-to-end digital case-management platform and associated configuration, integration, migration, implementation, transition and managed-service capability for the Subject Access Request Unit.
Subject Access Requests are the primary driver for the requirement, although the proposed solution is also expected to support Freedom of Information requests and Parliamentary Questions through configurable workflows and shared core case-management capability.
The proposed requirement may include:
- a configurable and secure case-management platform;
- customer submission and identity-verification capability or integration;
- configurable workflows, case administration, deadline management and auditability;
- document handling, processing and redaction capability;
- management information and operational reporting;
- integration with Authority-approved services, systems and platforms;
- configuration, implementation and testing;
- migration from existing systems and repositories;
- training, transition, go-live and service stabilisation;
- ongoing managed-service, support, maintenance, security and resilience; and
- exit and transition-out support.
The Authority currently intends to conduct a two-stage Competitive Flexible Procedure under the Procurement Act 2023. The final scope, procedure, timetable, contract term, estimated value and procurement documents remain subject to refinement and approval.

Cwm Taf Morgannwg UHB has entered a contract to Honeywell Building Solutions, via a direct award under Sections 41–43 and Schedule 5 of the Procurement Act 2023, based on:
• Exclusive technical and IP capability;
• Critical urgency to safeguard patient care and operational integrity;
• Compliance with statutory and governance obligations;
• Demonstrated value for money and sustainability outcomes.
Given the proprietary nature of the existing system, the urgent need to maintain critical hospital services, and the exclusive technical capability of Honeywell, it is appropriate and compliant under the Procurement Act 2023 for this contract to be awarded directly to Honeywell Building Solutions.

The provision of Tree Maintenance services covers Council owned trees within theboundaries of the London Borough of Hillingdon including;
Parks and Open Spaces,Woodlands,Housing estates,Tenant's gardens,Trees on Highways and Social Services establishments.
The provision of tree maintenance, will include, but is not necessarily restricted to;
Pruning,Felling,Stump removal, 24 hour per day, 365/7 days per year emergency call out duties,
This procurement is  split into the below Lots;
Lot 1: North Highways and Boroughwide out of hours call outs
Lot 2: South Highways
Lot 3: Housing and Corporate sites
The Contract will be for an initial term of three (3) years with an extendable option for a period of up to two (2) years, giving a total of five (5) years (the Term).

Scottish and Southern Electricity Networks (Distribution and Transmission) intends to establish a Framework Agreement for the supply, delivery and installation of cabinet solution. This will include; integration with PAC access control systems, commissioning, maintenance and support services.
SEN Distribution intends to replace 18 existing electronic key management cabinets that have reached end of life and are no longer supported, together with the installation of a further 4 new cabinets. 
SSEN Transmission has identified a requirement to deploy a new electronic key management solution across approximately 150 operational sites. 
The objective of this procurement is to establish a secure, cloud-based electronic key management solution that enables the safe storage, control and audit of keys used by Distribution and Transmission Engineering teams. The solution must integrate seamlessly with SSEN's existing PAC access control and security management systems and support efficient, secure access to critical operational assets.
The scope includes, but is not limited to, the need to provide a robust and highly available secure key storage solution, that is auditable, tamper resistant, and an operationally viable management solution for vehicle, substation, key site and operational technology cabinet access keys. Additionally, the service will need to ensure that it is aligned to industry best practice security controls and recognised standards such as Cyber Essentials, Cyber Essentials Plus, ISO 27001/2, ISA/IEC 62443 2-1, 2-4, 3-2 & 3-3 and NIST 800-53.
The solution needs to support:
- Secure and auditable key management and reporting;
- High availability (24 x 7 x 365) – failback solution – master key / break glass process / solution;
- Centrally managed and cloud hosted;
- Component based architecture and with integration capability out of the box to services such as PAC, CCTV and IDS; and
- Ease of use and operational management.
Successful Suppliers will be expected to demonstrate:
o	Understanding of recognised industry standards (Cyber Essentials, ISO27001/2,ISA/IEC 62443 2-1, 2-4, 3-2 & 3-3 and NIST 800-53);
o	A solution which is but not limited to a robust and highly available secure key storage solution;
o	Solution must be auditable and tamper resistant;
o	Solution must also be operationally viable for vehicle, substation, key site and operational technology cabinet key access;
o	High availability (24 x 7 x 365) – failback solution – master key / break glass process / solution;
o	Centrally managed and cloud hosted;
o	Component based architecture and with integration capability out of the box to services such as PAC, CCTV and IDS.

Below-threshold open competition
Blackwater & Hawley Town Council invites tenders for the design, supply, installation and commissioning of a new junior play area at:
Hawley Green, Fernhill Road, GU17 9BW
The works comprise the design, supply and installation of a Phase 1 junior play area primarily intended for children aged 7-12 years.
The project is expected to include, subject to tender proposals and available budget:
central multi-play unit;
climbing and traversing activities;
balancing activities;
elevated platforms;
slide(s);
spinner/roundabout;
zip wire/monorail;
multi-seat swing;
compliant safety surfacing;
foundations;
groundworks;
drainage where required;
installation;
testing and inspection;
certification; and
complete handover documentation.
The site is within a Conservation Area. Equipment must use a muted, natural colour palette.
Timber/wood-composite principal play equipment is not permitted.
The maximum equipment height shall be 4.0 metres.
The Phase 1 layout must allow for potential future expansion of the wider play area.
No perimeter fencing is included within Phase 1.
The successful contractor will be responsible for providing a complete and operational installation within the maximum contract value.
Funding
The Council is seeking £50,000 external grant funding towards the project.
The Council has agreed in principle to contribute £25,000 from reserves, conditional upon successful external grant funding and completion of all required Council financial and governance procedures.
The proposed total project value is therefore:
£75,000 exclusive of VAT.
Contract award is conditional upon confirmation of the required funding and all necessary Council approvals.
Additional Information
The Council encourages participation by suitably qualified small and medium-sized enterprises and specialist playground contractors.
Tenderers should ensure that their proposed solution provides the best combination of play value, quality, durability, safety, aesthetics and whole-life value within the maximum available budget.
Contract Award Conditions
Publication of this notice and the Invitation to Tender does not constitute a commitment by Blackwater & Hawley Town Council to award a contract.
Any contract award will be conditional upon:
successful external grant funding;
confirmation of the Council's contribution from reserves;
completion of tender evaluation;
satisfactory due diligence;
required Council approval;
availability of funding; and
agreement and execution of the final contract.
The Council reserves the right not to award the contract.

The Group intends to undertake a procurement exercise for the appointment of its external auditor and is conducting pre-market engagement to inform the development of its procurement strategy, specification and proposed approach to market.
The requirement will be for the provision of independent external audit services to the Group and relevant entities within the Group structure. The appointed auditor will be expected to deliver high-quality, independent and effective audit services in accordance with all applicable legislation, regulatory requirements, professional standards and relevant guidance.
The purpose of this pre-market engagement is to obtain views and information from suitably experienced organisations regarding the proposed requirement and the current external audit market. In particular, the Group is seeking to understand:
•	the capacity and capability of the market to deliver the proposed requirement;
•	the level and type of experience, expertise and resources required to deliver the services effectively;
•	appropriate approaches to audit planning, delivery, technology, data analytics and quality assurance;
•	mobilisation and transition requirements, particularly where there is a change of external auditor;
•	commercial and pricing considerations, including factors that may influence the cost of the services;
•	potential barriers to participation and opportunities to encourage effective competition; and
•	any other matters that the market considers relevant to the successful procurement and delivery of the services.

The Authority has a requirement to sustain and support a range of specialist mechanical, electro-mechanical, electrical, electronic, pneumatic and hydraulic equipment, including diving and atmosphere monitoring equipment, used in a marine environment. This is expected to include: 
•	Sourcing and procurement of items in support of equipment installation and modifications;
•	Provision of maintenance and repair services for items of equipment;
•	Provision of Post Design Engineering Services (PDES);
•	Obsolescence Management;
•	Maintenance of third-party Certification at equipment level;
•	Cleaning and packaging of equipment to an oxygen clean standard, typically DEFSTAN 68-278;
•	Packaging of equipment to DEFSTAN 81-41;
•	Support of equipment subject to International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR).

The University of Wales Trinity Saint David Group and Pembrokeshire College is establishing a multi-supplier Framework Agreement for the provision of property-related minor works, reactive maintenance and specialist trade services across its estate.
The Framework will provide the Client with a structured, compliant and efficient mechanism for procuring a range of planned, reactive and specialist works and services required to support the operation, maintenance and improvement of its estate.
The Framework is intended to support:
 Planned maintenance and minor works;
 Lifecycle replacement and improvement programmes;
 Compliance and statutory works;
 Minor refurbishment and alteration projects;
 Specialist trade works;
 Responsive and reactive maintenance;
 Urgent and emergency repairs and associated out-of-hours requirements.
The Framework is divided into fourteen Lots, with geographical coverage varying by Lot. Tenderers may apply for one or more Lots, subject to the requirements and geographical coverage specified within the Tender documentation.

The purpose of this Prior Information Notice (PIN) is to inform the supply market of the intention of the City of Edinburgh Council (the "Council") to establish a new Learning and Development Framework Agreement.
The Council's aim is to appoint a number of Learning Providers to deliver various learning requirements, including:
- First Aid;
- Epilepsy Awareness and Emergency Medication Administration (Buccal/oromuscular Midazolam), Anaphylaxis Awareness and Adrenaline Auto-Injector Administration;
- Manual Handling;
- Promoting Positive Engagement and Building Relationships;
- Mental Health Awareness and Mental Health First Aid;
- National Plant Operators Registration Scheme (NPORS);
- Driver Learning;
- The New Roads and Street Works Act 1991 (NRSWA Street Works);
- Strategic Leadership Development;
- Personal Safety; and
- Principles of Care.
It is expected that the new Framework shall operate from April 2027 for a period of two years, with the option to extend for up to a further two 12-month periods, undertaken at the sole discretion of the Council.
Learning Providers who may be interested in tendering for this opportunity are encouraged to note interest via Public Contracts Scotland. It is anticipated that the Contract Notice will be published in October 2026.
Please note that all information contained within this PIN, including the structure of this Framework and the proposed Lots, is under development and may be subject to change.
The Council's Survey can be located under Additional Documents. In addition to noting interest via Public Contracts Scotland, interested parties are asked to complete the Survey, indicating which Lot(s) are of interest to their organisation, and return it to Aga.Wieczorkiewicz@edinburgh.gov.uk
